// Module in Node.js (with small example):

A module in Node.js is a reusable file that contains code (functions, variables, objects) which can be exported and used in other files.

// data.js
const username = "Bhartee";
module.exports = username;

// app.js
const username = require("./data.js");
console.log(username);

➡️ This helps in code reusability, better organization, and clean structure.


// Types of Modules in Node.js (one-line with example):
Core Modules: Built-in Node.js modules (e.g., fs, http).
const fs = require("fs");

Local Modules: User-defined modules created in separate files.
const data = require("./data.js");

Third-Party Modules: External modules installed using npm.
const express = require("express");


// JavaScript Console vs Node.js Console

JavaScript (Browser) Console:
Runs inside the web browser
Used for frontend debugging
Has access to browser features like DOM, window, document
Code executes when the webpage loads or events occur

Node.js Console:
Runs in the system terminal/command prompt
Used for backend/server-side debugging
No access to DOM or browser objects
Code executes using the Node.js runtime